  "source": "content_cluster",
  "title": "Correcting deal price and volume errors in Sitara by email request",
  "task_class": "deal_entry_correction",
  "label_mechanical": "Deal entry and correction in Sitara / Unify / TAGG: correct deal / system / trading",
  "narrative": {
    "what_happens": "Someone checks a booked deal against what was actually confirmed or against a spreadsheet they circulated, finds the price or the scheduled volume in Sitara does not match, and emails whoever can edit the ticket to change it. The messages name the deal or ticket number, state what Sitara currently shows, and state what it should say \u2014 a fixed price instead of an index or tier price, a different index basis, or a corrected volume. The recipient makes the edit in the system and replies to confirm; in some threads the requester cannot make the change themselves because of access restrictions on the deal. It ends when the deal in the system matches the confirmation, which is asserted in mail rather than shown by any system output.",
    "where_it_stalls": "The correction cannot be made by the person who found it. One requester writes that they tried to open the Sitara ticket to fix swing pricing \"but I am not allowed to access it\", so the fix waits on a second party. Elsewhere the two sides disagree about the source of truth \u2014 a scheduled volume in Sitara against a volume in a spreadsheet the requester mailed \u2014 and the thread turns into asking whether there are conflicting numbers rather than a straight edit. Almost every message in this group carries a rework signal: the deal is being entered a second time by hand.",
    "why_it_recurs": "Deals are captured once from a confirmation and are only checked afterwards by eye. There is no automatic comparison between the confirmed terms and what Sitara holds, so each mismatch surfaces individually and is fixed by an email to someone with edit rights.",
    "automation_opportunity": "A scheduled reconciliation that compares confirmed price and volume against the Sitara record for each deal and emits an exception list to the desk, so discrepancies are found by the system rather than by reading. Paired with a standing change-request form carrying deal number, current value and corrected value, routed to whoever holds edit rights on that book.",
    "coherence": "tight"
  },
